Buyers of used battery electric vehicles (BEVs) lack reliable information about battery condition at purchase. This study provides the first willingness-to-pay (WTP) estimates for battery-health attributes in the U.S. used BEV market, using a discrete choice experiment (DCE) with 3,072 potential used-vehicle buyers. A mixed logit model shows consumers value 100 additional miles of range at \$11,070, penalize mileage ($-$\$2,780 per 10,000 miles), battery degradation ($-$\$980 per percentage-point of annual range loss), and refurbishment at the pack or cell level ($-$\$4,060 and $-$\$4,480). A latent class choice model further identifies six consumer segments, which differ substantially in their characteristics and sensitivity to battery-health attributes. Respondents who opted out of all choice tasks most often cited \textit{general BEV disinterest}, \textit{economic barriers}, and \textit{charging inconvenience}. Providing battery replacement cost and warranty information did not affect market engagement. The findings carry implications for certification programs, used-vehicle dealers, and OEMs.
